#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119528 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba9f608-c7bc-5eb6-ae4e-5f62cb7222a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are Different"}] :question/id #uuid "975987da-103c-5f24-93f3-56991b9e8b15" :question/math-academy-id "e-14" :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ What is $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\,-\\,4} f(x)?$\n\n![](images/ab/ab2abe13314c2005907dcd5d7077af8e87b7126474bbb0261f757d8ec7c00698.png)" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ grows to infinity, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4^+} f(x)=\\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function $f(x)$ is not defined for $x<-4,$ and consequently we cannot approach $x=-4$ from the left. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4^-} f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4} f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119535 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e021f949-0f55-50b8-8681-0b1ce71b21d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are the Same"}] :question/id #uuid "a9e611ae-68f1-5618-a53b-2c9b3606d468" :question/math-academy-id "e-15" :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\,4} f(x).$\n\n![](images/16/168433761f6629ad555a70acf5764f93246484c253f3c7ecba8fdfdfd87c2adf.png)" :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $4$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4^-} f(x) = -\\infty \\, .\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $4$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4^+} f(x) = -\\infty \\, .\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4} f(x)=-\\infty \\, .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e021f949-0f55-50b8-8681-0b1ce71b21d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are the Same"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1464e2dc-2eb3-5328-acbb-72144d143a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547356} {:answer/id #uuid "1b03f17c-9676-59e3-8db8-8753739f4a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547371} {:answer/id #uuid "a157332e-0d90-5a92-aec3-7773828f8e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547667} {:answer/id #uuid "beff4087-1730-533e-9082-c58aac5886b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547723} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9499a2-03af-5a9c-bb6e-bac7ee617297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b03f17c-9676-59e3-8db8-8753739f4a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547371} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eed7281-1934-5bdc-a678-4172938950d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "017219c2-ceac-5f2c-bcc8-c754086bffcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-102535" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f498fdcd8c6be58b8d1c1e6e4c3d835799a8c571cdd9125f7e7555307c94a6d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nWe see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is true.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e021f949-0f55-50b8-8681-0b1ce71b21d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are the Same"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9c0e29c-d52c-5258-ab57-b375f2f270e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9c0e29c-d52c-5258-ab57-b375f2f270e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547776} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e7ccd24-e09e-533b-9505-2417c732784e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2d9d433-ba35-5944-b803-b7c1dcd52280" :question/math-academy-id "q-338943" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e30b6d047b119e5beb5f0d5cf5e013628e7c9836e0ca264ee2da92d1e589a0fc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba9f608-c7bc-5eb6-ae4e-5f62cb7222a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are Different"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b496994b-a74b-5b4a-9928-66b5b1831571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b496994b-a74b-5b4a-9928-66b5b1831571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547708} :answer-field/id #uuid "b995a021-5404-518a-a21f-58d172fc8d4c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e817a4fb-b6c9-5bc7-b2be-45b00395342a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339105" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cdb3e9c74a36e5ef13436b7b18968064b278f6d8e0dffc27820f618f7f4fee9.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty $, and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba9f608-c7bc-5eb6-ae4e-5f62cb7222a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Infinite Limits when the Left and Right Limits are Different"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b12d8b2-9c93-5d39-a29d-a17f7189a787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547486} {:answer/id #uuid "5633242a-0afe-5bba-83cb-e91ca3e9b54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547508} {:answer/id #uuid "7b71a46f-d374-5dbd-89db-3d53441858bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547582} {:answer/id #uuid "8c73ec37-aac0-5246-86d7-04dd50677be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547626} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c110f7-40d7-5549-b3f0-eaba6fa2c482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c73ec37-aac0-5246-86d7-04dd50677be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547626} :answer-field/id #uuid "32f89007-7576-53ed-91a9-d7ed7308be7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50fa8042-1b8b-5648-8556-3cccc6bf538e" :question/math-academy-id "q-35921" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc6f40458a9a38615b9d1fe01c7e454611a56915d2dcaadaa8661c4d56defb6f.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function is not defined. So we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}]}
